  I think you have the answer there.  I just did a workup of missile
weapons for all roles.  Every role can get to at least basic with
daggers except Monks and Priests.  They still don't have a better
missile weapon than unskilled daggers.  

  (As an interesting side note, Basic+ knife skill would be better than
unskilled dagger, but the roles unskilled in dagger are also unskilled
with knives.)

  So thrown daggers for:
  - Common:
    - Easy to build up a supply early.
    - Can throw early and often.
      - Early throwing can make up for lack of multishot.
    - Discard those that get damaged.
  - Don't disappear.
    - This may be *the* major advantage.
    - Think of them as enchantable permanent rocks.
  - Relatively light.
  - Intrinsic +2 to-hit
    - Easier to advance (if you can).
    - Makes up for negative to hit bonus at unskilled.
  - Get the strength damage bonus.
  - No hands ranged weapon.
    - No need to wield a launcher.
    - Wield what you like.
  - Stacking.
    - Mass enchant.
    - Mass bless.
    - Saves inventory slots.
  - Enchantable.
  - There's a range of daggers available so dagger inventory
    can be continuously improved.
    - E.g. Elven daggers don't need to be made rustproof.

  The ability to use them for all roles greatly simplifies ranged weapon
player learning.  Multi-shot makes daggers a great close-in weapon as
well.  Multi-shot is also more important in the late game where
engagement range is more limited due to mazes and darkness.  (Light
sources give two ranged throws before a monster is at melee distance.)


  - Archaeologist, Barbarians, Cavemen, Knights, and Samurai can get to 
    Basic skill with daggers.
  - Healers can get to Skilled with daggers.  
  - Tourists, Valkyries and Wizards can get to Expert.  
  - Rangers and Rogues can get to Expert with Role bonus extra thrown 
    dagger.

  Even those roles (Monks and Priests) that are unskilled can hope for
one of the three artifact daggers as divine gifts to unlock dagger
skill. (Well, Neutral and Chaotic Monks and Priests can.)  That's what
started this whole discussion in the first place.



